.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt GETXATTR 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getxattr ,
.Nm setxattr ,
.Nm listxattr ,
.Nm removexattr
.Nd read and change extended attributes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/xattr.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn getxattr "const char *path" "const char *name" "void *value" "size_t size"
.Ft int
.Fn setxattr "const char *path" "const char *name" "const void *value" "size_t size" "int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn listxattr "const char *path" "char *list" "size_t size"
.Ft int
.Fn removexattr "const char *path" "const char *name"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An extended attribute is a name and a value kept with a file outside its
data.
The ext2 file system stores attributes whose names begin with
.Li user.
on regular files and directories; all names, values and their format
overhead must fit one file-system block, and other namespaces fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
but for the POSIX ACLs below.
A tmpfs keeps, in memory, attributes whose names begin with
.Li user. ,
.Li trusted.
or
.Li security.
on every kind of node, as Linux's tmpfs does, and lists them in the order
they were made; on a tmpfs with a limit of inodes each takes 40 bytes and
its name and value of the space that limit gives, 1024 bytes an inode, and
one that does not fit fails with
.Er ENOSPC .
Other namespaces fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
A value is an arbitrary sequence of bytes, including an empty sequence.
An attribute name is at most 255 bytes, not counting its terminating NUL.
Other file systems return an empty list and fail the other operations with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
.Fn getxattr
returns the value's length and copies its bytes to
.Fa value .
.Fn listxattr
returns the list's length and copies the names, each terminated by NUL.
For either call, a
.Fa size
of zero asks for the length alone; a nonzero buffer too small fails with
.Er ERANGE .
.Pp
.Fn setxattr
creates or replaces an attribute.
With
.Dv XATTR_CREATE
(1), an existing name fails with
.Er EEXIST ;
with
.Dv XATTR_REPLACE
(2), a missing name fails with
.Er ENODATA .
With both flags, an existing name is
.Er EEXIST
and a missing name
.Er ENODATA .
.Fn removexattr
removes an existing name, or fails with
.Er ENODATA .
.Pp
Reading a user attribute requires read permission, and changing one requires
write permission.
A trusted attribute is read, changed and listed by a privileged caller
alone; to another caller a read fails with
.Er ENODATA ,
a change with
.Er EPERM ,
and a listing leaves it out.
Anyone may read a security attribute, and only a privileged caller change
one, which is otherwise
.Er EPERM ,
as Linux's capability rules have it.
Only a sticky directory's owner or a privileged caller may change its
attributes.
User attributes on a symbolic link or a special file fail with
.Er ENODATA
when read and
.Er EPERM
when changed.
A change on an immutable or append-only inode fails with
.Er EPERM ;
a read-only mount fails with
.Er EROFS .
A successful change updates the change time and reports
.Dv IN_ATTRIB
to inotify.
.Pp
On an ext2 mount with POSIX ACLs, the names
.Li system.posix_acl_access
and
.Li system.posix_acl_default
are a file's access ACL and a directory's default ACL, in the form
.Li setfacl
writes: a 4-byte version, 2, then for each entry a 2-byte tag, 2-byte
permissions and a 4-byte id, little-endian, the id of a named user or group
in the caller's user namespace.
Every kind of file but a symbolic link keeps them, and
.Fn listxattr
names the ones a file has.
Anyone may read them.
Only the file's owner or a caller with
.Dv CAP_FOWNER
may set or remove one, which is otherwise
.Er EPERM ;
the flags are not read, an empty value removes the ACL, and removing an ACL
the file does not have succeeds.
An ACL whose entries are not in the order owner, named users, group, named
groups, mask, others, or that has a named entry and no mask, fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
and a value of another version with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
A default ACL on anything but a directory fails with
.Er EACCES .
Setting an access ACL sets the file's group permission bits to its mask's,
or, for an ACL the mode can say whole, sets the mode and keeps no ACL.
The access ACL then decides access for anyone but the owner, and a new file
takes its directory's default ACL in place of the umask.
On a symbolic link, or a mount without ACLs, both names fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
Each call first finds the file, so a path that names nothing fails as it
would for
.Xr stat 2 .
The
.Fn lgetxattr ,
.Fn lsetxattr ,
.Fn llistxattr
and
.Fn lremovexattr
forms act on a final symbolic link itself, and the
.Fn fgetxattr ,
.Fn fsetxattr ,
.Fn flistxattr
and
.Fn fremovexattr
forms on an open descriptor in place of
.Fa path .
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 226 to 237: set, get, list and remove,
each as the path, link and descriptor forms in that order.
On x86-64 they are 188 to 199, in the same order.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn getxattr
and
.Fn listxattr
return a byte count.
.Fn setxattr
and
.Fn removexattr
return 0.
A failure returns -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
The descriptor is not open, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A path, name or nonempty value buffer is inaccessible.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
.Fa path
names nothing.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A directory in
.Fa path
is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
The file system or namespace stores no extended attributes.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Unknown flags, an empty name after a namespace's prefix, or a malformed ACL.
.It Bq Er ENODATA
The requested attribute does not exist.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
.Dv XATTR_CREATE
found an existing attribute.
.It Bq Er ERANGE
A name exceeds 255 bytes or an output buffer is too small.
.It Bq Er E2BIG
A set value exceeds 65536 bytes.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The attributes do not fit a block, or no free block holds a new or shared-block replacement, on ext2; or a tmpfs has no inode space left for the value.
.It Bq Er EACCES
The file's permissions deny the operation, or a default ACL is set on a file
that is not a directory.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The file's type, sticky bit or inode flags forbid the change, a caller without privilege changes a trusted or security attribute, or a caller who neither owns the file nor holds CAP_FOWNER changes its ACL.
.It Bq Er EROFS
The mount is read-only.
.It Bq Er EIO
An attribute block is malformed or a disk request failed.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
There is no memory for private buffers.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr chattr 8
